Phase 6A §2.1: real per-service memory at idle.

$ docker stats --no-stream --format "table {{.Name}}\t{{.MemUsage}}\t{{.MemPerc}}"
NAME                            MEM USAGE / LIMIT     MEM %
ledgerlight-phase0-postgres-1   2.339GiB / 7.355GiB   31.80%
ledgerlight-phase0-garage-1     94.77MiB / 7.355GiB   1.26%

Context: these two containers had been running continuously for 8+
hours at the time of this measurement, spanning the whole multi-phase
session (Phase 5 §8 through Phase 6A §1) -- postgres's 2.339GiB figure
is real accumulated shared_buffers/page-cache usage over that uptime,
not a fresh-start baseline. A fresh `docker compose up` would start
measurably lower; this is the real number this long-lived dev instance
actually shows right now, which is the figure that matters for
deciding whether this host has real headroom for the heavier e2e-lite
run.

api/worker/web were not running at measurement time (no built images
exist locally -- removed after Phase 5's own §5/§7 verification,
confirmed via `docker images`) -- no idle figures to report for them
yet; they will be measured on first real build+start under the
e2e-lite profile.

Real host memory at the time of this measurement (`Get-CimInstance
Win32_OperatingSystem`): 0.46GB free out of 15.2GB total -- critically
low, from the user's own other running applications (Firefox's many
processes alone measured ~4.3GB; vmmemWSL, this project's own Docker
VM, measured 1.14GB real total, consistent with the two containers
above, not the source of the pressure). Flagged directly to the user
via AskUserQuestion rather than attempting a build under this
constraint; the user chose to pause the heavy steps (build, Playwright,
ZAP) until memory headroom improves, and to proceed with the safe,
execution-free parts of §2 in the meantime (this measurement, and the
e2e-lite compose override file itself).

UPDATE (same session, once memory recovered): the e2e-lite stack was
actually brought up and the real Playwright suite run against it.

Idle, right after a fresh `docker compose ... up -d --build postgres
garage api web` under the e2e-lite profile (`docker stats
--no-stream`):
```
NAME                            MEM USAGE / LIMIT   MEM %
ledgerlight-phase0-web-1        51.98MiB / 512MiB   10.15%
ledgerlight-phase0-api-1        148.3MiB / 512MiB   28.97%
ledgerlight-phase0-postgres-1   26.18MiB / 512MiB    5.11%
ledgerlight-phase0-garage-1     8.098MiB / 128MiB    6.33%
```
(A fresh postgres start, not the 8+-hour-accumulated-cache figure
above -- confirms the e2e-lite tuning works: 26MiB vs 2.3GiB.)

Under real e2e load (immediately after a full, 4-test Playwright run
-- login, search, overview, timeline, evidence, investigation
creation through to FINALISE, dossier view, review decision,
watchlist/alert-rule creation, webhook registration/test, alert
visibility, cross-tenant denial):
```
NAME                            MEM USAGE / LIMIT   MEM %
ledgerlight-phase0-web-1        105.1MiB / 512MiB   20.53%
ledgerlight-phase0-api-1        361.4MiB / 512MiB   70.59%
ledgerlight-phase0-postgres-1   57.54MiB / 512MiB   11.24%
ledgerlight-phase0-garage-1     26.21MiB / 128MiB   20.47%
```
`api`'s real jump (148MB -> 361MB) is the real NLI/verifier model
loading during the investigation's own real VERIFY step -- the
single largest real memory consumer, still comfortably inside its
512MB e2e-lite budget. Total real footprint across all four services
under load: ~550MB, well inside this host's real available headroom
on this specific occasion.

Real host memory during this work was NOT itself stable -- it swung
from 2.37GB free down to as low as 0.29GB and back up to 1.66GB+
across the session (from the user's own other applications, not this
stack), causing 2 real Docker build kills along the way (recorded
separately in `sec02-web-rebuild-csp-fix.log`/`...-fix2.log`). The
e2e-lite stack's OWN footprint, once up, stayed small and stable
throughout; the instability was in getting a *build* to complete
under a host-wide squeeze, not in running the resulting stack.

ZAP baseline: not yet run -- see docs/phase-6/progress.md for current
status.
